  m_Name: eye_of_newt
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::Data.ScriptableObjects.StoryBookDefinition
  Id: eye_of_newt
  Title: Eye of Newt
  Author: Bramble Underleaf
  Color: {r: 0.55, g: 0.75, b: 0.4, a: 1}
  Hint: On the western shore, where the grass meets the sand
  Pages:
  - 'Every potion book in the land calls for an eye of newt, and every newt in the
    land is very tired of hearing about it.


    None more so than Nettlebrook,
    a small spotted newt who lived under a flat stone at the edge of the pond. He
    was fond of his eyes. He used them for looking at things, and there were a great
    many things worth looking at.'
  - 'Each spring the young witches came down to the bank with their baskets, and
    each spring every newt in the pond hid in the mud until they had gone.


    "It''s
    no way to live," said Nettlebrook, the year he turned seven. "Somebody ought
    to ask."


    The other newts agreed that somebody ought to. Then they all looked
    at him.'
  - 'So Nettlebrook set off across the pond to see Granny Sedge, the oldest witch
    on the island, who lived in a boathouse on the far bank.


    It was a long
    way for a newt. He crossed on the lily pads, one wobbly hop at a time, while
    the frogs sunbathing on them made remarks.


    "Off to volunteer, are we?"
    croaked the biggest. "Very brave. Very crunchy."'
  - 'Granny Sedge was shelling peas on her doorstep. Nettlebrook climbed the step,
    drew himself up as tall as a newt can, and said, "Madam. Why newts?"


    She
    peered at him over her spectacles. "Why newts what, dear?"


    "Why our eyes?
    What have we ever done to anybody?"'
  - 'Granny Sedge laughed so hard her hat fell off and floated away down the pond.


    "Oh,
    you poor dear," she wheezed. "Eye of newt is mustard seed! It''s only an old
    name. Our grandmothers gave every herb in the garden a dreadful name, to keep
    their recipes secret. Nobody wants your eyes. Nobody has ever wanted your eyes."'
  - 'She fetched a dusty book and showed him the list. Eye of newt: mustard seed.
    Wool of bat: holly leaves. Tongue of dog: houndstongue, a perfectly nice little
    flower.


    Nettlebrook read down the page, then read it again, then pointed
    with one small hand.


    "And toe of frog?"


    "Buttercup," said Granny
    Sedge. "Why?"


    "No reason," said Nettlebrook.'
  - 'He took the long way home, along the bank, so that he could pass the lily pads
    very slowly.


    "Lovely afternoon," he called up to the biggest frog. "I''ve
    just been reading about potions. Fascinating stuff. Do you happen to know what
    toe of frog is?"


    The frog went very pale, for a frog, and did not sunbathe
    again for a week.'
  - 'That night the newts threw a party that lasted three days. There was dancing
    on the stones, singing in the reeds and a great deal of pondweed cake.


    The
    frogs, who were not invited, sulked loudly from the lily pads the whole time.
    On the fourth morning Nettlebrook went over and told them about the buttercups,
    because a joke is only funny until somebody cries.'
  - 'The frogs were so relieved that they threw a party of their own, and this time
    everybody came.


    To this day the newts keep a jar of mustard seed by the
    water''s edge, labelled in very careful handwriting: EYE OF NEWT (NOT REALLY).
    HELP YOURSELF.


    The frogs keep a jar of buttercups beside it. Theirs says:
    TOE OF FROG. ALSO NOT REALLY. PLEASE.'
